<!-- S03 CONSTELLATIONS -->
<div class="section">
<div class="section-header"><span class="section-number">03</span><h2>Major Constellation &amp; Operator Breakdown</h2></div>
<div class="section-body">
<p style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:13px;margin-bottom:1rem;">
SpaceX's Starlink constellation dominates the active satellite population, accounting for
<strong style="color:#fff">67.8% of all active satellites</strong>. The remaining ~32% is split across
commercial, civil, and government operators. This extreme concentration fundamentally skews orbital
statistics toward LEO and low-inclination shells.
</p>
<div class="chart-grid">
<div><div class="chart-title">Top 15 Operators by Satellite Count</div><div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chart-operators"></canvas></div></div>
<div><div class="chart-title">Starlink Shell Inclination Breakdown (n=10,083)</div><div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chart-starlink-inc"></canvas></div></div>
</div>
<div style="margin-top:1.5rem;"><div class="chart-title">Non-Starlink Operators &mdash; Share of Remaining 4,792 Satellites</div><div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chart-ops-excl-starlink"></canvas></div></div>
<div class="callout" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
<strong>Starlink Shell Strategy.</strong> SpaceX operates multiple orbital shells differentiated by inclination: the 53.2&deg; shell (~4,840 sats) covers mid-latitude global coverage; the 40&deg; shell (~3,656 sats) adds polar coverage; and the 97.6&deg; shell (~872 sats) provides high-latitude reinforcement. Shells at 70&deg; (~506 sats) and 65&deg; (~209 sats) address regional coverage gaps. This multi-shell approach makes Starlink's inclination distribution multimodal, producing the overall LEO median inclination of ~53&deg;.
</div>
</div>
</div>

<!-- S04 LAUNCH TRENDS -->
<div class="section">
<div class="section-header"><span class="section-number" style="color:var(--accent);border-color:var(--accent);">04</span><h2 style="color:var(--accent);">Launch Year Trends &mdash; The Great Acceleration</h2></div>
<div class="section-body">
<p style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:13px;margin-bottom:1rem;">
Of the 14,875 active satellites, <strong style="color:#fff">13,461 (90.5%) were launched since 2020</strong>.
The 2025 active fleet alone accounts for 4,329 satellites &mdash; more than the combined total of all
spacecraft launched before 2018. The growth from 498 (2020) to 4,329 (2025) represents an 8.7&times;
increase in five years.
</p>
<div style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;"><div class="chart-title">Active Satellites by Launch Year &mdash; Full History (1964&ndash;2026)</div><div class="chart-wrap" style="height:320px;"><canvas id="chart-launch-full"></canvas></div></div>
