<!-- S06 CONCLUSIONS -->
<div class="section">
<div class="section-header"><span class="section-number">06</span><h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</h2></div>
<div class="section-body">
<ul class="takeaway-list">
<li>
<div class="takeaway-num">1</div>
<div class="takeaway-text">
<strong>Mega-constellations have fundamentally restructured Earth orbit.</strong>
A single operator &mdash; SpaceX Starlink &mdash; accounts for 67.8% of all active satellites.
The top five operators (Starlink, OneWeb, Planet, Cosmos, Iridium) represent ~74% of the active population.
Spectrum allocation and orbital congestion policy cannot be discussed without Starlink as the central case.
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="takeaway-num">2</div>
<div class="takeaway-text">
<strong>LEO is the operational frontier.</strong> With 86.5% of active satellites in LEO, medium and high Earth orbit regimes remain relatively uncongested. The crowding challenge is almost exclusively a LEO problem &mdash; concentrated in two inclination bands (40&deg;&ndash;60&deg;) at altitudes between ~550 km and ~1,200 km.
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="takeaway-num">3</div>
<div class="takeaway-text">
<strong>The launch growth curve is near-vertical since 2020.</strong> From 498 active satellites launched in 2020 to 4,329 in 2025 &mdash; an 8.7&times; increase in five years. This rate of change outpaces the revision cycles of ITU spectrum regulations and national licensing frameworks.
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="takeaway-num">4</div>
<div class="takeaway-text">
<strong>Debris risk is increasingly a Starlink-specific concern.</strong> With 10,083 active Starlink satellites, SpaceX's own conjunction management practices effectively define the debris environment in heavily used shells. The FCC's 2022 five-year post-mission disposal rule for LEO satellites below 2,000 km is a significant step, but enforcement and international harmonization remain open questions.
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="takeaway-num">5</div>
<div class="takeaway-text">
<strong>Inclination stratification creates distinct congestion zones.</strong> Starlink's multi-shell strategy (40&deg;, 53&deg;, 65&deg;, 70&deg;, 97&deg; shells) deliberately fills different latitudinal coverage bands &mdash; but specific inclination corridors face disproportionate conjunction density. Operators planning new constellations face a de facto zoning problem: certain shells are becoming crowded while others remain available.
</div>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
