</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="takeaway-num">4</div>
<div class="takeaway-text">
<strong>Debris risk is increasingly a Starlink-specific concern.</strong> With 10,083 active Starlink satellites, SpaceX's own conjunction management practices effectively define the debris environment in heavily used shells. The FCC's 2022 five-year post-mission disposal rule for LEO satellites below 2,000 km is a significant step, but enforcement and international harmonization remain open questions.
</div>
</li>
<li>
<div class="takeaway-num">5</div>
<div class="takeaway-text">
<strong>Inclination stratification creates distinct congestion zones.</strong> Starlink's multi-shell strategy (40&deg;, 53&deg;, 65&deg;, 70&deg;, 97&deg; shells) deliberately fills different latitudinal coverage bands &mdash; but specific inclination corridors face disproportionate conjunction density. Operators planning new constellations face a de facto zoning problem: certain shells are becoming crowded while others remain available.
</div>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
